{
  "_note": "Breed -> body-type lookup for the buddy creator's step 1 search. The buddy is always a PUPPY, so every mapping means the puppy build of that breed, not the adult. Body type is derived from `group` via groupBody; a breed carries an explicit `body` ONLY where it breaks its group, and each of those carries a `why`. That keeps ~370 rows reviewable: you check the group rule once, then read the exceptions.",
  "_sources": [
    "Groupings follow FCI nomenclature and AKC groups, collapsed to the eight silhouettes the creator offers.",
    "Aliases are the names people actually type -- kennel-club spellings, common shortenings, slang and frequent misspellings."
  ],

  "_groupBodyNote": "Twelve authoring groups collapse onto the eight body types in components/buddy.js. More groups than body types on purpose: `scenthound` and `sporting` share a silhouette but not a set of exceptions, and merging them would lose that.",
  "groupBody": {
    "toy": "toy",
    "companion": "toy",
    "terrier": "terrier",
    "spitz": "spitz",
    "primitive": "spitz",
    "sporting": "sporting",
    "scenthound": "sporting",
    "sighthound": "sighthound",
    "herding": "herding",
    "working": "mastiff",
    "molosser": "mastiff",
    "low_set": "low_set"
  },

  "_crossesNote": "What a cross commonly produces BEYOND its parents' own types. Most pairs add nothing -- the parents' two types are the answer. Short legs (low_set) are the notable exception: the trait is dominant, so a low-set parent crossed with anything tends to throw low-set pups, and a tester looking for their husky-corgi should find low_set offered whichever parent they think of first. Keys are the two body types sorted alphabetically and joined with +.",
  "crosses": {
    "low_set+spitz": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+sporting": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+herding": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+terrier": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+mastiff": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+toy": ["low_set"],
    "low_set+sighthound": ["low_set"],
    "mastiff+toy": ["terrier"],
    "mastiff+sighthound": ["sporting"]
  },

  "_fillersNote": "Stripped before matching, so 'half husky half corgi', 'husky x corgi', 'husky/corgi mix' and 'part husky part corgi puppy' all resolve to the same two breeds.",
  "fillers": ["half", "part", "mostly", "mix", "mixed", "cross", "crossed", "crossbreed", "x", "and", "with", "of", "a", "an", "the", "my", "puppy", "pup", "dog", "breed", "type", "percent", "%"],

  "_genericNote": "Someone who types 'mutt' knows their dog is a mix and nothing more. Offering all nine is the honest answer -- narrowing would be a guess.",
  "generic": ["mutt", "mongrel", "mixed breed", "heinz 57", "rescue", "shelter dog", "street dog", "pariah", "crossbreed", "designer dog", "unknown", "no idea", "not sure"],

